    "beforeQuestion": "Before this lesson starts, what does Emotional regulation strategies mean to me?",
    "learningPurpose": "Help parents and carers understand emotional regulation strategies and practise one safe, child-centred response that can be used in daily life.",
    "learningOutcomes": [
      "Explain what Emotional regulation strategies means in safe, practical parenting language.",
      "Identify one child need, feeling, risk, skill gap or developmental factor connected to the topic.",
      "Practise one calm, clear and child-centred response.",
      "Create one evidence item showing reflection, planning or behaviour change."
    ],
    "teachingTranscript": "This lesson focuses on calming the body, naming emotions and responding to stress before problem solving.\n\nIn this lesson, Emotional regulation strategies is treated as a practical parenting skill: something the adult can notice, practise, record and improve over time. The aim is not to complete the topic perfectly in one session. The aim is to slow the moment down, understand what the child may need, and choose one response that is safer than the old pattern.\n\nStart by noticing the situation without labelling the child. Ask: What happened? What did the child feel, need or struggle with? What did I do? What could I do next time that would be calmer, clearer or safer? This reflection helps move the adult from reaction to intentional parenting.\n\nCore skills for this lesson are: name the feeling, slow the body before teaching, reduce stimulation where possible, and model calm breathing and grounding. These skills should be practised in small daily moments, not only during crisis. Children trust repeated behaviour more than promises.\n\nUse the skill in an ordinary daily moment so progress becomes practical, observable and repeatable.\n\nProgress can be recorded through a short reflection, a routine plan, a communication example, a calm repair statement, a completed checklist, or a note about what was practised and what changed.",
    "lessonPlan": "time: 0-5 minutes\nsegment: Start questions\ntask: Answer the three start questions honestly and briefly.\n\ntime: 5-12 minutes\nsegment: Teach\ntask: Read or listen to the lesson transcript and identify the main parenting skill.\n\ntime: 12-22 minutes\nsegment: Activity 1\ntask: Complete Notice the Pattern.\n\ntime: 22-34 minutes\nsegment: Activity 2\ntask: Complete Practise the Safer Response.\n\ntime: 34-37 minutes\nsegment: Quiz\ntask: Complete the quick knowledge check.\n\ntime: 37-40 minutes\nsegment: Reflection and evidence\ntask: Answer the reflection questions and choose one evidence item to save or upload.",
    "practiceActivities": "name: Activity 1: Notice the Pattern\ndurationMinutes: 10\ninstructions: Write one recent or realistic example connected to Emotional regulation strategies. Record the trigger, the child’s likely feeling or need, the adult response, and one safer response to practise next time.\nevidencePrompt: Save the completed pattern note as a reflection or learning record.\n\nname: Activity 2: Practise the Safer Response\ndurationMinutes: 12\ninstructions: Create one sentence, routine, limit, repair statement or practical action that applies Emotional regulation strategies in daily life. Keep it short enough to use when stressed.\nevidencePrompt: Upload the sentence, plan, checklist, script or routine change you created.",
    "knowledgeCheck": "question: A dysregulated child usually learns better after their body has calmed.\noptions: True\n\nFalse\nanswer: True\nexplanation: Calming the nervous system improves listening, thinking and repair.\n\nquestion: The main aim of Emotional regulation strategies is to practise one safer response, not to become perfect in one session.\noptions: True\n\nFalse\nanswer: True\nexplanation: SAFE STEPS lessons focus on practical progress, reflection and repeated safe action.\n\nquestion: Evidence of change can include reflection notes, routines, plans, repair statements, checklists or examples of safer communication.\noptions: True\n\nFalse\nanswer: True\nexplanation: Evidence can show effort, insight, follow-through and changed behaviour over time.",
