    "teachingTranscript": "This lesson focuses on practical daily care, routines and health habits that support children’s regulation and wellbeing.\n\nIn this lesson, Food Allergies and Intolerances is treated as a practical parenting skill: something the adult can notice, practise, record and improve over time. The aim is not to complete the topic perfectly in one session. The aim is to slow the moment down, understand what the child may need, and choose one response that is safer than the old pattern.\n\nStart by noticing the situation without labelling the child. Ask: What happened? What did the child feel, need or struggle with? What did I do? What could I do next time that would be calmer, clearer or safer? This reflection helps move the adult from reaction to intentional parenting.\n\nCore skills for this lesson are: make the routine predictable, reduce pressure and shame, prepare the environment before the problem occurs, and seek qualified health advice when safety or growth is a concern. These skills should be practised in small daily moments, not only during crisis. Children trust repeated behaviour more than promises.\n\nAvoid shame-based food talk. Seek qualified health advice for allergies, infant feeding, growth concerns, restrictive eating or medical issues.\n\nProgress can be recorded through a short reflection, a routine plan, a communication example, a calm repair statement, a completed checklist, or a note about what was practised and what changed.",
