    "beforeQuestion": "Before this lesson starts, what does Healthy growth mean to me?",
    "learningPurpose": "Help parents and carers understand healthy growth and practise one safe, child-centred response that can be used in daily life.",
    "learningOutcomes": [
      "Explain what Healthy growth means in safe, practical parenting language.",
      "Identify one child need, feeling, risk, skill gap or developmental factor connected to the topic.",
      "Practise one calm, clear and child-centred response.",
      "Create one evidence item showing reflection, planning or behaviour change."
    ],
    "teachingTranscript": "This lesson focuses on practical daily care, routines and health habits that support children’s regulation and wellbeing.\n\nIn this lesson, Healthy growth is treated as a practical parenting skill: something the adult can notice, practise, record and improve over time. The aim is not to complete the topic perfectly in one session. The aim is to slow the moment down, understand what the child may need, and choose one response that is safer than the old pattern.\n\nStart by noticing the situation without labelling the child. Ask: What happened? What did the child feel, need or struggle with? What did I do? What could I do next time that would be calmer, clearer or safer? This reflection helps move the adult from reaction to intentional parenting.\n\nCore skills for this lesson are: make the routine predictable, reduce pressure and shame, prepare the environment before the problem occurs, and seek qualified health advice when safety or growth is a concern. These skills should be practised in small daily moments, not only during crisis. Children trust repeated behaviour more than promises.\n\nUse the skill in an ordinary daily moment so progress becomes practical, observable and repeatable.\n\nProgress can be recorded through a short reflection, a routine plan, a communication example, a calm repair statement, a completed checklist, or a note about what was practised and what changed.",
    "lessonPlan": "time: 0-5 minutes\nsegment: Start questions\ntask: Answer the three start questions honestly and briefly.\n\ntime: 5-12 minutes\nsegment: Teach\ntask: Read or listen to the lesson transcript and identify the main parenting skill.\n\ntime: 12-22 minutes\nsegment: Activity 1\ntask: Complete Notice the Pattern.\n\ntime: 22-34 minutes\nsegment: Activity 2\ntask: Complete Practise the Safer Response.\n\ntime: 34-37 minutes\nsegment: Quiz\ntask: Complete the quick knowledge check.\n\ntime: 37-40 minutes\nsegment: Reflection and evidence\ntask: Answer the reflection questions and choose one evidence item to save or upload.",
    "practiceActivities": "name: Activity 1: Notice the Pattern\ndurationMinutes: 10\ninstructions: Write one recent or realistic example connected to Healthy growth. Record the trigger, the child’s likely feeling or need, the adult response, and one safer response to practise next time.\nevidencePrompt: Save the completed pattern note as a reflection or learning record.\n\nname: Activity 2: Practise the Safer Response\ndurationMinutes: 12\ninstructions: Create one sentence, routine, limit, repair statement or practical action that applies Healthy growth in daily life. Keep it short enough to use when stressed.\nevidencePrompt: Upload the sentence, plan, checklist, script or routine change you created.",
    "knowledgeCheck": "question: Predictable daily routines can support regulation and wellbeing.\noptions: True\n\nFalse\nanswer: True\nexplanation: Routines reduce uncertainty and help children know what comes next.\n\nquestion: The main aim of Healthy growth is to practise one safer response, not to become perfect in one session.\noptions: True\n\nFalse\nanswer: True\nexplanation: SAFE STEPS lessons focus on practical progress, reflection and repeated safe action.\n\nquestion: Evidence of change can include reflection notes, routines, plans, repair statements, checklists or examples of safer communication.\noptions: True\n\nFalse\nanswer: True\nexplanation: Evidence can show effort, insight, follow-through and changed behaviour over time.",
    "evidenceTask": "Upload or write one short evidence item showing how you practised Healthy growth. This may be a reflection, routine plan, safety action, communication script, checklist, repair statement or daily-life example.",
