    "standardDurationMinutes": 35,
    "beforeQuestion": "Before this lesson starts, what does Individual differences mean to me?",
    "learningPurpose": "Help parents and carers understand individual differences and practise one safe, child-centred response that can be used in daily life.",
    "learningOutcomes": [
      "Explain what Individual differences means in safe, practical parenting language.",
      "Identify one child need, feeling, risk, skill gap or developmental factor connected to the topic.",
      "Practise one calm, clear and child-centred response.",
      "Create one evidence item showing reflection, planning or behaviour change."
    ],
    "teachingTranscript": "This lesson builds realistic expectations by linking parenting responses to age, stage, development and learning needs.\n\nIn this lesson, Individual differences is treated as a practical parenting skill: something the adult can notice, practise, record and improve over time. The aim is not to complete the topic perfectly in one session. The aim is to slow the moment down, understand what the child may need, and choose one response that is safer than the old pattern.\n\nStart by noticing the situation without labelling the child. Ask: What happened? What did the child feel, need or struggle with? What did I do? What could I do next time that would be calmer, clearer or safer? This reflection helps move the adult from reaction to intentional parenting.\n\nCore skills for this lesson are: check whether the expectation matches the child’s age and capacity, break skills into smaller steps, model first and practise repeatedly, and adjust support for individual needs. These skills should be practised in small daily moments, not only during crisis. Children trust repeated behaviour more than promises.\n\nUse the skill in an ordinary daily moment so progress becomes practical, observable and repeatable.\n\nProgress can be recorded through a short reflection, a routine plan, a communication example, a calm repair statement, a completed checklist, or a note about what was practised and what changed.",
    "lessonPlan": "time: 0-5 minutes\nsegment: Start questions\ntask: Answer the three start questions honestly and briefly.\n\ntime: 5-12 minutes\nsegment: Teach\ntask: Read or listen to the lesson transcript and identify the main parenting skill.\n\ntime: 12-22 minutes\nsegment: Activity 1\ntask: Complete Notice the Pattern.\n\ntime: 22-34 minutes\nsegment: Activity 2\ntask: Complete Practise the Safer Response.\n\ntime: 34-37 minutes\nsegment: Quiz\ntask: Complete the quick knowledge check.\n\ntime: 37-40 minutes\nsegment: Reflection and evidence\ntask: Answer the reflection questions and choose one evidence item to save or upload.",
