    "teachingTranscript": "This lesson builds firm, kind behaviour guidance that teaches skills without fear, shame or harshness.\n\nIn this lesson, Logical Consequences - Accountability is treated as a practical parenting skill: something the adult can notice, practise, record and improve over time. The aim is not to complete the topic perfectly in one session. The aim is to slow the moment down, understand what the child may need, and choose one response that is safer than the old pattern.\n\nStart by noticing the situation without labelling the child. Ask: What happened? What did the child feel, need or struggle with? What did I do? What could I do next time that would be calmer, clearer or safer? This reflection helps move the adult from reaction to intentional parenting.\n\nCore skills for this lesson are: describe the behaviour without labels, set one clear limit, teach the replacement behaviour, and follow through calmly and consistently. These skills should be practised in small daily moments, not only during crisis. Children trust repeated behaviour more than promises.\n\nUse the skill in an ordinary daily moment so progress becomes practical, observable and repeatable.\n\nProgress can be recorded through a short reflection, a routine plan, a communication example, a calm repair statement, a completed checklist, or a note about what was practised and what changed.",
