    "teachingTranscript": "Lesson title: Secure vs Insecure Attachment\n\nQuick question before this lesson: Before this lesson starts, what does Secure vs Insecure Attachment mean to me?\n\nThis lesson turns Secure vs Insecure Attachment into a practical parenting skill that can be noticed, practised and recorded in daily life.\n\nChildren build trust through repeated experiences of safety, repair and predictable care. Small moments of connection often matter more than big promises.\n\nStart by thinking about where Secure vs Insecure Attachment shows up in your home. It may appear during routines, discipline, communication, health care, school mornings, contact visits, co-parenting exchanges, or moments when a child has big feelings. The key is to slow down enough to notice the pattern before reacting.\n\nA safe parenting response usually has four parts: pause, check the child’s need, choose a calm response, and follow through consistently. This does not mean letting children do whatever they want. It means guiding behaviour without fear, shame or confusion.\n\nFor this topic, choose one real example from your family life. Write down what happened, what the child may have needed, what you did, and what you could try next time. This creates evidence of insight and change because it shows thinking, planning and follow-through.\n\nFinish the lesson by choosing one action you can practise during the next week. Keep it small enough to repeat. A small action done consistently is stronger evidence than a large plan that is not used.",
