    "teachingTranscript": "Lesson title: Parent - Child Negotiation\n\nQuick question before this lesson: Before this lesson starts, what does Parent - Child Negotiation mean to me?\n\nThis lesson turns Parent - Child Negotiation into a practical parenting skill that can be noticed, practised and recorded in daily life.\n\nCommunication works best when the adult stays calm, uses clear words and listens for the need underneath the behaviour. The aim is not to win the conversation; the aim is to keep the relationship safe while solving the problem.\n\nStart by thinking about where Parent - Child Negotiation shows up in your home. It may appear during routines, discipline, communication, health care, school mornings, contact visits, co-parenting exchanges, or moments when a child has big feelings. The key is to slow down enough to notice the pattern before reacting.\n\nA safe parenting response usually has four parts: pause, check the child’s need, choose a calm response, and follow through consistently. This does not mean letting children do whatever they want. It means guiding behaviour without fear, shame or confusion.\n\nFor this topic, choose one real example from your family life. Write down what happened, what the child may have needed, what you did, and what you could try next time. This creates evidence of insight and change because it shows thinking, planning and follow-through.\n\nFinish the lesson by choosing one action you can practise during the next week. Keep it small enough to repeat. A small action done consistently is stronger evidence than a large plan that is not used.",
