    "teachingTranscript": "Lesson title: Relationship Building\n\nQuick question before this lesson: Before this lesson starts, what does Relationship Building mean to me?\n\nThis lesson turns Relationship Building into a practical parenting skill that can be noticed, practised and recorded in daily life.\n\nChildren build trust through repeated experiences of safety, repair and predictable care. Small moments of connection often matter more than big promises.\n\nStart by thinking about where Relationship Building shows up in your home. It may appear during routines, discipline, communication, health care, school mornings, contact visits, co-parenting exchanges, or moments when a child has big feelings. The key is to slow down enough to notice the pattern before reacting.\n\nA safe parenting response usually has four parts: pause, check the child’s need, choose a calm response, and follow through consistently. This does not mean letting children do whatever they want. It means guiding behaviour without fear, shame or confusion.\n\nFor this topic, choose one real example from your family life. Write down what happened, what the child may have needed, what you did, and what you could try next time. This creates evidence of insight and change because it shows thinking, planning and follow-through.\n\nFinish the lesson by choosing one action you can practise during the next week. Keep it small enough to repeat. A small action done consistently is stronger evidence than a large plan that is not used.",
    "lessonPlan": "time: 0-5 minutes\nsegment: Opening title and quick question\ntask: Display the lesson title. Answer the quick question before reading the lesson.\n\ntime: 5-12 minutes\nsegment: Teach\ntask: Read or listen to the lesson transcript and identify the main parenting skill.\n\ntime: 12-22 minutes\nsegment: Activity 1\ntask: Complete Notice the Pattern.\n\ntime: 22-34 minutes\nsegment: Activity 2\ntask: Complete Practise the Safer Response.\n\ntime: 34-37 minutes\nsegment: Quiz\ntask: Complete the quick knowledge check.\n\ntime: 37-40 minutes\nsegment: Reflection and evidence\ntask: Answer the reflection questions and choose one evidence item to save or upload.",
    "practiceActivities": "title: Activity 1: Notice the Pattern\ndurationMinutes: 10\ninstructions: Write one recent situation where Relationship Building showed up.\n\nDescribe what happened using neutral, observable words.\n\nIdentify what the child may have been feeling, needing or trying to communicate.\n\nWrite one thing the adult response did well and one thing that could be improved.\nrecordAsEvidence: true\n\ntitle: Activity 2: Practise the Safer Response\ndurationMinutes: 12\ninstructions: Choose the same situation or a likely future situation.\n\nWrite a calm first sentence you could use with the child.\n\nWrite the limit, choice, repair step or support you would offer.\n\nWrite how you will follow through without threats, yelling, shame or giving up.\nrecordAsEvidence: true",
    "knowledgeCheck": "question: What is the main aim of learning about Relationship Building?\noptions: To control the child quickly\n\nTo understand the issue and choose a safer, more consistent response\n\nTo prove the parent is always right\n\nTo avoid all boundaries\nanswerIndex: 1\nexplanation: The aim is practical insight and safer parenting behaviour, not control or blame.\n\nquestion: What should a parent do before reacting to difficult behaviour?\noptions: Pause and consider the child’s need, feeling or skill gap\n\nRaise their voice so the child knows it is serious\n\nIgnore every behaviour\n\nUse a consequence without explanation\nanswerIndex: 0\nexplanation: A short pause helps the parent respond rather than react.\n\nquestion: What makes evidence of parenting change stronger?\noptions: One perfect statement\n\nRepeated small examples of reflection, planning and follow-through\n\nOnly saying the child was difficult\n\nAvoiding written records\nanswerIndex: 1\nexplanation: Consistent evidence over time shows real behaviour change.\n\nquestion: Which response is most child-centred?\noptions: Shaming the child so they remember\n\nLetting the child set every rule\n\nStaying calm, naming the issue and guiding the next step\n\nWithdrawing connection until the child complies\nanswerIndex: 2\nexplanation: Child-centred parenting keeps safety, connection and teaching together.\n\nquestion: What should the parent choose at the end of the lesson?\noptions: One small action to practise this week\n\nA long plan with no follow-through\n\nA list of everything the child does wrong\n\nA promise to never feel stressed\nanswerIndex: 0\nexplanation: A small repeatable action is realistic and measurable.",
    "evidenceTask": "title: Evidence task: Relationship Building practice record\ninstructions: Record one real example of practising Relationship Building this week.\n\nInclude the date, what happened, what you noticed, what response you chose and what changed afterwards.\n\nUpload a short written reflection, routine plan, communication script, photo of a routine chart, or other safe evidence item if appropriate. Do not upload private information about the child that is not needed.\nevidenceType: reflection_or_practical_record\nrecommendedUpload: Short written reflection or practical plan",
