    "teachingTranscript": "Lesson title: Separated Parents in Family Court\n\nQuick question before this lesson: Before this lesson starts, what does Separated Parents in Family Court mean to me?\n\nThis lesson turns Separated Parents in Family Court into a practical parenting skill that can be noticed, practised and recorded in daily life.\n\nIn a court or child protection context, this topic is not about sounding perfect. It is about showing safe insight, realistic planning and consistent child-focused behaviour over time.\n\nStart by thinking about where Separated Parents in Family Court shows up in your home. It may appear during routines, discipline, communication, health care, school mornings, contact visits, co-parenting exchanges, or moments when a child has big feelings. The key is to slow down enough to notice the pattern before reacting.\n\nA safe parenting response usually has four parts: pause, check the child’s need, choose a calm response, and follow through consistently. This does not mean letting children do whatever they want. It means guiding behaviour without fear, shame or confusion.\n\nFor this topic, choose one real example from your family life. Write down what happened, what the child may have needed, what you did, and what you could try next time. This creates evidence of insight and change because it shows thinking, planning and follow-through.\n\nFinish the lesson by choosing one action you can practise during the next week. Keep it small enough to repeat. A small action done consistently is stronger evidence than a large plan that is not used.",
